/* book: lead: No source emits one pure wavelength: a DFB laser spans a fraction of a nanometre, an LED tens of nanometres. Each wavelength in a pulse travels at its own group velocity, so the pulse arrives stretched. This is chromatic (intramodal) dispersion, and it happens even in a single-mode fiber. The fiber is described by a dispersion coefficient D in ps/(nm·km); the spread it causes, Δτ, is a time in ps. */
/* book Figure 2.6.1: Material dispersion of the glass crosses zero near 1270 nm; the negative waveguide part pulls the total zero to 1310 nm. At 1550 nm the parts are +21.0 and −3.5, giving D = 17.5 ps/(nm·km), the value of worked example 2. */
