/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Light energy travels at the group velocity vg = dω/dβ, where β is the propagation constant of the mode and ω the angular frequency.
   - D is the dispersion coefficient in ps/(nm·km); τ is the group delay in ps; L is in km; λ is in nm.
   - Δτ is the pulse spread in ps; D in ps/(nm·km); L in km; Δλ is the spectral width of the source in nm.
   - This book takes D > 0 when the longer wavelengths arrive later (the “anomalous” region of standard fiber at 1550 nm).
   - Dm is the material dispersion and Dw the waveguide dispersion, both in ps/(nm·km).
   - The index of silica falls as wavelength rises, and the curve n(λ) bends.
   - λ is the wavelength in m; c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s; d²n/dλ² is the curvature of the index curve in m⁻².
   - For pure silica Dm crosses zero near 1270 nm; germania in the core moves this a little longer.
   - In a single-mode fiber part of the power travels in the cladding, and that share depends on the normalised frequency V = (2πa/λ)√(n₁² − n₂²), where a is the core radius.
   - For a standard step-index single-mode fiber Dw is negative, about −4 ps/(nm·km) across 1300 to 1600 nm.
*/
