/* book Worked example: Q: A 100 km single-mode link runs at 1550 nm with D = 17 ps/(nm·km). (a) Find the spread for a laser of spectral width 0.1 nm and the bit-rate limit B ≤ 1/(2Δτ). (b) Repeat for a 2 nm Fabry–Perot laser. (c) What does an LED of 40 nm width give?
   1. (a) Δτ = 17 × 100 × 0.1 = 170 ps. B ≤ 1/(2 × 170 × 10⁻¹² s) = 2.94 × 10⁹ bit/s = 2.94 Gbit/s.
   2. (b) Δτ = 17 × 100 × 2 = 3400 ps = 3.4 ns. B ≤ 1/(2 × 3.4 × 10⁻⁹) = 1.47 × 10⁸ bit/s = 147 Mbit/s.
   3. A twentyfold wider spectrum gives a twentyfold wider spread and a twentyfold lower limit.
   4. (c) Δτ = 17 × 100 × 40 = 68 000 ps = 68 ns, so B ≤ 1/(2 × 68 × 10⁻⁹) = 7.35 Mbit/s.
   Answer: 170 ps and 2.94 Gbit/s with the 0.1 nm laser; 3.4 ns and 147 Mbit/s with the 2 nm laser; 68 ns and 7.4 Mbit/s with the LED. Wide sources belong on short links. */
