/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Drawing and cabling leave the core slightly non-circular and add stress that is not the same in all directions.
   - Birefringence makes the polarization state change along the fiber.
   - LB is the beat length in m; λ is the wavelength in m; δn is the birefringence (a pure number).
   - In a short piece of fiber whose axes stay fixed, the delay between the two polarizations, the differential group delay (DGD), grows in proportion to length: Δτ ≈ L δn/c when the dispersion of δn is ignored.
   - In an installed cable the principal axes do not stay fixed.
   - ⟨Δτ⟩ is the mean DGD in ps; DPMD is the PMD coefficient in ps/√km; L is the length in km.
   - Modern fiber has DPMD of about 0.02 to 0.1 ps/√km; cable laid before the late 1990s can exceed 0.5 ps/√km.
   - A common design rule for direct-detection links keeps the mean DGD below a tenth of the bit period:
   - both sides in ps; Tb = 1/B is the bit period, 100 ps at 10 Gbit/s and 25 ps at 40 Gbit/s.
   - PMD differs from chromatic dispersion in three ways: it scales as √L, not L; it does not depend on the source spectral width; and it changes with time, so it cannot be cancelled by a fixed spool of fiber.
*/
