/* book exam traps: Using Δτ proportional to L for a cable. Linear growth holds only for a short piece with fixed axes; a real cable gives DPMD√L. | Writing DPMD in ps/km. Its unit is ps/√km. | Comparing the mean DGD with the whole bit period. The usual rule uses one tenth of it. | Treating the mean as the worst case. The DGD is random and its Maxwellian tail exceeds twice the mean 1.7 % of the time. | Thinking a narrower laser reduces PMD. PMD does not depend on the spectral width; chromatic dispersion does. */
